    {
      "@id": "concept:law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011-sch-2-sec-34",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "34 Judge or magistrate acting oppressively or when interested",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011#sch-2-sec-34"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011.pdf, p.16",
      "values:originalText": "Excessive and unreasonable bail\n\n(1) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a judge or magistrate; and\n\n(b) the judge or magistrate is required or authorised by law to\nadmit a person accused of an offence to bail; and\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2011A00003\n\nAdministration of justice offences  Schedule 2\nAmendment of the Crimes Act 1914  Part 1\n\nLaw and Justice Legislation Amendment (Identity Crimes and Other Measures) Act 2011       No.\n3, 2011            13\n\n(c) the judge or magistrate requires excessive and unreasonable\nbail; and\n\n(d) the requirement is an abuse of the judge’s or magistrate’s\noffice; and\n\n(e) the offence referred to in paragraph (b) is an offence against a\nlaw of the Commonwealth.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 2 years.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply if the judge or magistrate has a\nreasonable excuse.\nNote:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (2) (see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code).\n\n(3) Absolute liability applies to the paragraph (1)(e) element of the\noffence.\nNote:\nFor absolute liability, see section 6.2 of the Criminal Code.\nActing when interested\n\n(4) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a judge or magistrate; and\n\n(b) the judge or magistrate perversely exercises jurisdiction in a\nmatter; and\n\n(c) the judge or magistrate has a personal interest in the matter;\nand\n\n(d) the jurisdiction is federal jurisdiction.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 2 years.\n\n(5) Absolute liability applies to the paragraph (4)(d) element of the\noffence.\nNote:\nFor absolute liability, see section 6.2 of the Criminal Code.",
      "skos:definition": "Excessive and unreasonable bail\n\n(1) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a judge or magistrate; and\n\n(b) the judge or magistrate is required or authorised by law to\nadmit a person accused of an offence to bail; and\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2011A00003\n\nAdministration of justice offences  Schedule 2\nAmendment of the Crimes Act 1914  Part 1\n\nLaw and Justice Legislation Amendment (Identity Crimes and Other Measures) Act 2011       No.\n3, 2011            13\n\n(c) the judge or magistrate requires excessive and unreasonable\nbail; and\n\n(d) the requirement is an abuse of the judge’s or magistrate’s\noffice; and\n\n(e) the offence referred to in paragraph (b) is an offence against a\nlaw of the Commonwealth.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 2 years.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply if the judge or magistrate has a\nreasonable excuse.\nNote:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (2) (see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code).\n\n(3) Absolute liability applies to the paragraph (1)(e) element of the\noffence.\nNote:\nFor absolute liability, see section 6.2 of the Criminal Code.\nActing when interested\n\n(4) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a judge or magistrate; and\n\n(b) the judge or magistrate perversely exercises jurisdiction in a\nmatter; and\n\n(c) the judge or magistrate has a personal interest in the matter;\nand\n\n(d) the jurisdiction is federal jurisdiction.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 2 years.\n\n(5) Absolute liability applies to the paragraph (4)(d) element of the\noffence.\nNote:\nFor absolute liability, see section 6.2 of the Criminal Code.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011-sch-2-sec-34-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011-sch-2-sec-34-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011-sch-2-sec-34-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011-sch-2-sec-34-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011-sch-2-sec-34-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "f0628f8e7a89f74ba8b122220552de8147d539081ed033e91f115824339b4f06"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011#sch-2-sec-34",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "34 Judge or magistrate acting oppressively or when interested",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 16,
      "values:originalText": "Excessive and unreasonable bail\n\n(1) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a judge or magistrate; and\n\n(b) the judge or magistrate is required or authorised by law to\nadmit a person accused of an offence to bail; and\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2011A00003\n\nAdministration of justice offences  Schedule 2\nAmendment of the Crimes Act 1914  Part 1\n\nLaw and Justice Legislation Amendment (Identity Crimes and Other Measures) Act 2011       No.\n3, 2011            13\n\n(c) the judge or magistrate requires excessive and unreasonable\nbail; and\n\n(d) the requirement is an abuse of the judge’s or magistrate’s\noffice; and\n\n(e) the offence referred to in paragraph (b) is an offence against a\nlaw of the Commonwealth.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 2 years.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply if the judge or magistrate has a\nreasonable excuse.\nNote:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (2) (see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code).\n\n(3) Absolute liability applies to the paragraph (1)(e) element of the\noffence.\nNote:\nFor absolute liability, see section 6.2 of the Criminal Code.\nActing when interested\n\n(4) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a judge or magistrate; and\n\n(b) the judge or magistrate perversely exercises jurisdiction in a\nmatter; and\n\n(c) the judge or magistrate has a personal interest in the matter;\nand\n\n(d) the jurisdiction is federal jurisdiction.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 2 years.\n\n(5) Absolute liability applies to the paragraph (4)(d) element of the\noffence.\nNote:\nFor absolute liability, see section 6.2 of the Criminal Code."
    },
