    {
      "@id": "concept:law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011-sch-2-sec-45a",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "45A Criminal detention definitions",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011#sch-2-sec-45a"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011.pdf, p.25",
      "values:originalText": "In this Act:\ncriminal detention: a person is in criminal detention if the person:\n\n(a) is arrested in respect of an offence; or\n\n(b) is in custody in respect of an offence; or\n\n(c) is detained because:\n\n(i) the person is unfit to be tried in respect of an offence; or\n\n(ii) the person has been acquitted of an offence because of\nmental illness at the time of the offence; or\n\n(iii) the person has been convicted of an offence and a court\nhas ordered that the person be detained for the purposes\nof receiving treatment for a mental illness that\ncontributed to the commission of the offence.\nExample: Paragraph (c) includes detention under:\n(a) Division 6 of Part IB (unfitness to be tried); or\n(b) Division 7 of Part IB or section 20BS (mental illness).\nfederal criminal detention means criminal detention in respect of\nan offence against a law of:\n\n(a) the Commonwealth; or\n\n(b) a Territory (other than the Australian Capital Territory).",
      "skos:definition": "In this Act:\ncriminal detention: a person is in criminal detention if the person:\n\n(a) is arrested in respect of an offence; or\n\n(b) is in custody in respect of an offence; or\n\n(c) is detained because:\n\n(i) the person is unfit to be tried in respect of an offence; or\n\n(ii) the person has been acquitted of an offence because of\nmental illness at the time of the offence; or\n\n(iii) the person has been convicted of an offence and a court\nhas ordered that the person be detained for the purposes\nof receiving treatment for a mental illness that\ncontributed to the commission of the offence.\nExample: Paragraph (c) includes detention under:\n(a) Division 6 of Part IB (unfitness to be tried); or\n(b) Division 7 of Part IB or section 20BS (mental illness).\nfederal criminal detention means criminal detention in respect of\nan offence against a law of:\n\n(a) the Commonwealth; or\n\n(b) a Territory (other than the Australian Capital Territory).",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011-sch-2-sec-45a-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011-sch-2-sec-45a-logic-deontic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011-sch-2-sec-45a-logic-descriptionlogic-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "13e1ddae326b05717c8ccbefec9984df4794eef471064ef054f423afecb76b48",
      "skos:note": "Definition of criminal detention"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/law-and-justice-legislation-amendment-identity-crimes-and-other-measures-act-2011#sch-2-sec-45a",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "45A Criminal detention definitions",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 25,
      "values:originalText": "In this Act:\ncriminal detention: a person is in criminal detention if the person:\n\n(a) is arrested in respect of an offence; or\n\n(b) is in custody in respect of an offence; or\n\n(c) is detained because:\n\n(i) the person is unfit to be tried in respect of an offence; or\n\n(ii) the person has been acquitted of an offence because of\nmental illness at the time of the offence; or\n\n(iii) the person has been convicted of an offence and a court\nhas ordered that the person be detained for the purposes\nof receiving treatment for a mental illness that\ncontributed to the commission of the offence.\nExample: Paragraph (c) includes detention under:\n(a) Division 6 of Part IB (unfitness to be tried); or\n(b) Division 7 of Part IB or section 20BS (mental illness).\nfederal criminal detention means criminal detention in respect of\nan offence against a law of:\n\n(a) the Commonwealth; or\n\n(b) a Territory (other than the Australian Capital Territory)."
    },
